x86_64-linux  there is nothing to compare here, by construction. A case that includes a C++ standard library header is compiled by clang against *clang's* library, not this one - so the two objects hold the symbols of two different implementations, and on the cross targets clang has no C++ headers at all and refuses the file outright. The oracle for these cases is behaviour, not names: run.sh hands the same program to both compilers on all three machines and compares what it prints, which is the stronger question and the one that found the returned-temporary fault.
arm64-darwin  there is nothing to compare here, by construction. A case that includes a C++ standard library header is compiled by clang against *clang's* library, not this one - so the two objects hold the symbols of two different implementations, and on the cross targets clang has no C++ headers at all and refuses the file outright. The oracle for these cases is behaviour, not names: run.sh hands the same program to both compilers on all three machines and compares what it prints, which is the stronger question and the one that found the returned-temporary fault.
